<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Emirate of Multan</h1><p class="st-meta">855 CE – 1010 CE (approx.) · capital Multan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Arab emirate of the upper Indus, first Sunni and later Ismaili, ruling the rich pilgrimage city of Multan until Mahmud of Ghazni took it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-qasim">Muhammad ibn Qasim</a> in 713. As Abbasid control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> weakened, an Arab family, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-munabbih">Banu Munabbih</a>, claiming descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a>, ruled it from around 855 as an autonomous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> that recognised the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan's</a> famous Sun temple drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> pilgrims from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, according to al-Masudi, used its idol as a hostage against Hindu attack and drew revenue from the offerings. In the 950s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> missionaries of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> won over the ruling class, and under the Ismaili ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalam-ibn-shayban">Jalam ibn Shayban</a> the Sun temple was destroyed and a mosque built in its place. Multan traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-fath-dawud">Abu al-Fath Dawud</a> in 1006 and annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> around 1010, persecuting the Ismailis. Multan became a province of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire</a> and later a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/emirate-of-multan.jpg" alt="Emirate of Multan" width="320" height="296"><figcaption>Image: Map created from DEMIS Mapserver, which are public domain. Koba-chan.Contouring and city names: Cattette · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Multan_Emirate.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>855 CE – 1010 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Multan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jalam ibn Shayban, Abu al-Fath Dawud</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam, then Ismaili Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Sindhi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Banu Munabbih of Multan, Ismaili Multan, Qarmatians of Multan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=932&amp;focus=emirate-of-multan" aria-label="Emirate of Multan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6535 1075 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Emirate of Multan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6855 1331L6134 1224M6855 1331L5811 1337M6855 1331L6748 1217"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6134 1224h0M5811 1337h0M6748 1217h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6855 1331h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6855 1331h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Multan. <a href="/atlas?year=932&amp;focus=emirate-of-multan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
